::CW_U03_L05_Q01::Passage: The narrator describes a cracked cup each time the family avoids talking about moving. What is the writer's likely purpose?{
~A. To explain how cups are made.#Incorrect. The passage is not informational.
=B. To make the cup a visible sign of family tension.#Correct. The repeated cup connects object detail to emotional conflict.
~C. To remove tension from the scene.#Incorrect. The repeated image builds tension.
~D. To show that moving is never discussed anywhere.#Incorrect. It is discussed indirectly through avoidance.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q02::Passage: "Naturally, the committee chose the plan that solved nothing and cost twice as much." What rhetorical choice is strongest?{
~A. Neutral description.#Incorrect. The wording has judgment.
~B. Celebration.#Incorrect. The sentence criticizes the plan.
~C. Scientific definition.#Incorrect. It is not defining a term.
=D. Sarcasm.#Correct. "Naturally" creates a critical, ironic attitude.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q03::Passage: The writer delays revealing why the narrator will not enter the house until the last paragraph. What is the likely reader effect?{
=A. It creates suspense and curiosity.#Correct. Delayed explanation makes readers wonder.
~B. It proves houses are unimportant.#Incorrect. The house matters.
~C. It removes structure.#Incorrect. Delay is a structure choice.
~D. It makes the ending impossible to understand.#Incorrect. It can clarify later.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q04::Passage: "The trophy glittered under the lights, but Mira kept looking at the empty seat in the front row." What craft choice supports purpose?{
~A. A recipe list.#Incorrect. No recipe appears.
~B. Random weather detail.#Incorrect. No weather appears.
=C. Contrast between public success and private absence.#Correct. The trophy and empty seat create emotional contrast.
~D. Removing all emotion.#Incorrect. The contrast creates emotion.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q05::Passage: A writer wants readers to distrust a narrator. Which choice best supports that purpose?{
=A. The narrator gives three different versions of the same event.#Correct. Contradiction creates doubt.
~B. The narrator uses paragraphs.#Incorrect. Paragraphs do not create distrust by themselves.
~C. The narrator names a color once.#Incorrect. That is not enough.
~D. The narrator describes lunch accurately.#Incorrect. Accuracy does not create distrust.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q06::Passage: The writer uses a gentle tone when describing a rival's mistake. What purpose might this serve?{
~A. To make the rival seem monstrous.#Incorrect. Gentle tone does the opposite.
~B. To remove reader response.#Incorrect. Tone shapes response.
~C. To explain grammar only.#Incorrect. This is a craft effect.
=D. To invite sympathy instead of ridicule.#Correct. Gentle tone guides a compassionate reader response.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q07::Passage: "The hallway narrowed with every step toward the principal's office." What reader effect does the image create?{
~A. It provides exact architecture measurements.#Incorrect. It is figurative perception.
=B. It makes anxiety feel physical.#Correct. The hallway image reflects pressure.
~C. It creates a happy celebration.#Incorrect. The image feels tense.
~D. It removes the setting.#Incorrect. The hallway is setting.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q08::Passage: A writer repeats the phrase "not yet" as a character postpones telling the truth. What is the purpose of repetition?{
~A. To make the scene unrelated.#Incorrect. It connects to character action.
~B. To prove the character already confessed.#Incorrect. The character postpones confession.
=C. To emphasize delay and avoidance.#Correct. Repetition mirrors repeated postponement.
~D. To avoid rhetoric.#Incorrect. Repetition is rhetorical.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q09::Passage: The scene opens with the final argument, then moves backward to show the small choices that caused it. What structural purpose is likely?{
~A. To remove cause and effect.#Incorrect. The flashback shows causes.
~B. To make time irrelevant.#Incorrect. Time structure matters.
~C. To list unrelated events.#Incorrect. The events explain the conflict.
=D. To make readers ask how the conflict reached that point.#Correct. Starting at the argument creates a question.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q10::Passage: "She smiled so widely that her brother stepped back." What does the detail likely do?{
=A. It hints that the smile may hide anger or pressure.#Correct. The brother's reaction complicates the smile.
~B. It proves the scene has no tension.#Incorrect. Stepping back suggests tension.
~C. It explains dentistry.#Incorrect. No dental information appears.
~D. It removes character response.#Incorrect. The brother responds.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q11::Passage: A writer wants to show grief without naming it. Which craft choice best fits?{
~A. Say "She was sad" five times.#Incorrect. That names emotion directly and bluntly.
~B. Add unrelated sports scores.#Incorrect. That distracts.
=C. Show the character setting two plates at dinner, then putting one back.#Correct. The action implies absence and grief.
~D. Remove all action.#Incorrect. Action can show emotion.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q12::Passage: The narrator uses formal, distant language while describing a painful memory. What reader effect might this create?{
~A. It always means the memory is funny.#Incorrect. Formal distance does not guarantee humor.
=B. It can show emotional restraint or avoidance.#Correct. Distance in language can reveal controlled pain.
~C. It removes voice.#Incorrect. Formal distance is a voice choice.
~D. It proves the narrator is not affected.#Incorrect. It may show avoidance.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q13::Passage: The writer gives the antagonist one quiet moment helping a child. What purpose might this serve?{
=A. To complicate the reader's view of the character.#Correct. A humane moment adds complexity.
~B. To prove the character has no role.#Incorrect. It deepens the role.
~C. To remove all conflict.#Incorrect. Complexity can increase conflict.
~D. To switch genres automatically.#Incorrect. One moment does not determine genre.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q14::Passage: "The clock clicked louder each time Ren avoided the question." What craft choice is used?{
~A. A map legend.#Incorrect. No map appears.
~B. A direct definition.#Incorrect. The sentence uses sensory detail.
~C. Comic relief only.#Incorrect. The detail creates tension.
=D. Sound detail to increase tension.#Correct. The ticking sound emphasizes delay.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q15::Passage: A writer wants the reader to feel trapped with the character. Which choice best supports this purpose?{
~A. Describe an unrelated picnic.#Incorrect. This distracts from feeling trapped.
~B. Summarize the scene in one neutral sentence.#Incorrect. That weakens the immersive effect.
=C. Use close narration and repeated details of locked exits.#Correct. Close narration and recurring locked exits create confinement.
~D. Use no setting details.#Incorrect. Setting details can create trapped feeling.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q16::Passage: The author includes dialogue where every answer is shorter than the question before it. What effect can this create?{
~A. It always shows joy.#Incorrect. It more likely shows tension or guardedness.
=B. It can show a character becoming guarded or unwilling to speak.#Correct. Shorter answers suggest withdrawal.
~C. It proves no character is present.#Incorrect. Dialogue means characters are speaking.
~D. It removes pacing.#Incorrect. Dialogue length affects pacing.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q17::Passage: "The apology sat in my drafts folder all week." What does the object detail do?{
~A. It explains how email servers work.#Incorrect. Technology mechanics are not the focus.
~B. It proves the apology was spoken.#Incorrect. It remains in drafts.
~C. It removes conflict.#Incorrect. Hesitation creates conflict.
=D. It makes hesitation visible.#Correct. The unsent draft shows delayed action.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q18::Passage: The writer uses second person: "You keep your hand on the doorknob, pretending you are only checking the lock." What is a likely purpose?{
=A. To place the reader inside the character's anxious self-deception.#Correct. "You" creates immediacy.
~B. To make the scene a recipe.#Incorrect. It remains narrative.
~C. To reveal every character's thoughts.#Incorrect. Second person does not do that automatically.
~D. To remove setting.#Incorrect. The doorknob is setting detail.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q19::Passage: A writer wants readers to notice injustice in a school rule. Which rhetorical choice fits?{
~A. Avoid all examples.#Incorrect. Examples help readers see the issue.
=B. Show the rule affecting two students unfairly in the same scene.#Correct. Concrete contrast can reveal injustice.
~C. Add unrelated weather only.#Incorrect. Weather alone does not show injustice.
~D. Make every character agree with the rule.#Incorrect. That may hide conflict.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q20::Passage: "No one said the word leaving, but every box was labeled in thick black marker." What purpose does the detail serve?{
~A. It proves nobody can read.#Incorrect. The labels are readable.
~B. It makes moving impossible.#Incorrect. The boxes suggest moving.
=C. It shows the truth of leaving without direct dialogue.#Correct. The boxes reveal what characters avoid saying.
~D. It explains marker chemistry.#Incorrect. The marker is a visual detail.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q21::Passage: Which answer best connects craft choice to purpose?{
=A. The writer slows the moment before the confession so readers feel the character's fear.#Correct. It names choice and effect.
~B. The writer has words because stories have words.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. The story is long because it is long.#Incorrect. This explains nothing.
~D. The character exists in the passage.#Incorrect. Existence is not craft analysis.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q22::Passage: The author describes the town celebration through a character who feels excluded. What purpose might point of view serve?{
~A. It proves celebrations are impossible.#Incorrect. The celebration exists.
~B. It reveals every townsperson's thoughts.#Incorrect. The focus is one character.
=C. It makes the celebration feel painful instead of joyful.#Correct. Perspective changes reader experience.
~D. It removes tone.#Incorrect. Perspective shapes tone.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q23::Passage: A writer cuts a paragraph of explanation and adds one image of a packed suitcase by the door. What purpose might this serve?{
~A. To make the passage less visual.#Incorrect. It adds a visual image.
~B. To remove meaning.#Incorrect. The image carries meaning.
~C. To explain suitcase manufacturing.#Incorrect. Not the purpose.
=D. To show departure through concrete detail instead of telling.#Correct. The suitcase implies leaving.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q24::Passage: "The mayor called the crumbling bridge 'a minor inconvenience' while drivers turned around one by one." What rhetorical effect is created?{
~A. The bridge disappears.#Incorrect. It remains central.
=B. Irony exposes the mayor's understatement.#Correct. The bridge problem contradicts "minor inconvenience."
~C. Everyone agrees there is no problem.#Incorrect. Drivers turn around.
~D. The sentence has no tone.#Incorrect. It has critical tone.
}
::CW_U03_L05_Q25::Passage: A student says, "The author used a detail because details are required." What feedback is best?{
=A. Explain what the detail makes readers think, feel, notice, or question.#Correct. Purpose analysis needs reader effect.
~B. Keep the answer because it is specific enough.#Incorrect. It is too general.
~C. Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is needed.
~D. Focus only on punctuation.#Incorrect. The task is craft purpose.
}
